Date: Sun, 26 Dec 93 22:20:46 -0800
From: Romana Machado <romana@apple.com>
Subject: Why have children?

It appears that Reilly Jones and I have radically different
interpretations of Extropian philosophy.
 
>Oliver Seiler writes: <Well, unpredictability and reducing
>the risk of genetically inherited incidences of cancer and
>such can go hand in hand.>
>
>Agreed.  No sense creating horribly genetically diseased
>children if it is preventable.
 
Excellent. I consider stupidity a "horrible preventable
disease", and claim that there is scientific evidence that
well-designed genes could help to fight it.
 
>  Designer children concepts will tend towards the eugenics
>path with poor results for society and boring results for
>the parents who raise the children.
 
Well-designed genes, available on the free market, would
make transhuman children possible, with possibly devastating
results for the State, and quite positive and interesting
results for the parents, when they are free to raise the
 best children that current technology will allow.
 
>  By far the bulk of personality traits in children are
>formed from family relationships and society at large, not
>genetically.  Neuronal plasticityin the early child-raising
>years is well documented.  The social brain has
>equalimportance with the individual brain.
 
Genetic selection can ensure that your children will have
strong bodies and brains. Parents can start with a solid
foundation. A congenital idiot's "neuronal plasticity"
doesn't help him much.
 
>  David Friedman writes: <That creates a niche for women
>who are good at producing babies to get inseminated by very
>high quality fathers--sperm being essentially a free good--
>in order to produce children for adoption.>
>
>  Yuck!  A man and a woman should get married if they want
>a child and stay married while raising the child.
 
Why do you find this disgusting? Do I hear an antiquated
Judeo-Christian attitude?
 
>  The loving connection of two parents and child, supported
>by pro-family societal values, does more to create healthy,
>intelligent people than any genetic tinkering or screening
>or adoption schemes ever will.  The bulk of the self is
>epigenetically derived.
 
How about the loving connection of *four* parents and child?
or *three* or *one*? Is it just the _love connection_ that
counts, or are you arguing that number and gender of parents
counts too? Can you present evidence that supports this
claim? Extropians are generally suspicious of arguments
about "societal values" because they are often invoked to
justify the persecution of individuals, especially in cases
of consensual crime.
 
Rudy Rucker wrote:"An acorn is a program that takes a
hundred years to run; its output is an oak tree."  Master
gardeners know that flower seeds differ in quality; they buy
the best they can get. They also know that their
responsiblity does not stop there; plants must be cared for
if they are to give the best bloom. Though they often agree,
sometimes they have different opinions about what the "best
care" is, and still produce good results.
 
>  Genes are stupid, people are smart.  Selling genes on the
>market is weird.
 
People who pay attention to genes are smart.
Selling genes on the market is a rational and extropian
activity that will make some smart people plenty of money.
Buying genes rationally can also produce a desirable
outcome. People who want things more "natural" should be
warned that nature devised mosquitos and polio; humans
devised repellents and vaccines.
 
>  No one is beating down the sperm bank doors to be
>potential fathers to children they will never have the
>opportunity to create socially.
 
 
No one at all?
Presently, some parents have sought out superior sperm by
purchasing from a Nobel Prize winner's sperm bank; I believe
all parties acted well. (Besides, remember that crooked
insemination doctor who shot up all his patients with his
own semen?) When it is directly profitable, well advertised,
and legal, I am sure that more men (and women, if tech
catches up) will be interested.
 
Intelligent use of technology can increase children's health
and intelligence. Consumers should be free of social or
legal restrictions on the use of technology. "Social values"
should have nothing to do with individual reproductive
freedom. Extropians recognize the irrational basis of moral
disgust.
 
