Date: Mon, 27 Dec 93 00:24:39 PST
From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: BIOLOGY: Freezing Eggs

I said:
> Is freezing one's eggs a possible option for a healthy woman aged
> puberty-35 today if she doesn't want to have children now, but may
> want to in 10-20 years?  

Mike Price:
> ...  But if you _did_ have your head (or eggs or almost any
> tissue) frozen your full genome would be preserved.  Tinkering with the
> gene control and expression mechanisms (realistically 10-30 years, IMO)
> would enable the culturing of fully viable ova or whatever.  

Kennita Watson:
>If I'm not mistaken, an egg doesn't contain the mother's full genome;
>I thought that was part of the point of sexual reproduction.

Mike Price:
>True, my error.  I was thinking of recovering the original genome from
>multiple soma cells (like neurons - that's my Alcor neurosuspension bias
>showing, I guess!).  DNA is recovered from mummies and the like by
>splicing together broken strands from different cells.  The principle
>could be used to recover the full complement from a small sample of ova,
>although with more difficulty.

There's a little bit of confusion here about full-genomes, DNA etc.
The reason that you can't take a non-egg cell like a nose-cell (a la Sleeper),
extract the DNA from the nucleus, and make a cell suitable for reproduction is
1) the nose-cell has already been expressed (biologists call this 
kind of cell "differentiated") as a nose-cell, and 2) you only want 1/2 of
the mother's DNA for sexual reproduction (as Kennita pointed out). If you
tried to create a human from the DNA from a nose-cell nucleus, you would just
get another nose! The nose-cell has already synthesized different sets of
proteins from its chemical environment. 

Here's a quick explanation:
Two cells with the same genetic code perform different tasks because the
cells are selective as to *which* of their instructions they actually use.
They may make the proteins specified by some of their genes and not those
specified by others of their genes. One way to exert such selectivity is
to transcribe some genes to synthesize messenger RNA along the DNA of 
some genes, but not of others. 

The flow of DNA information proceeds in the following way: The DNA of the 
gene determines the mRNA, which determines protein enzymes, which control
chemical reactions, which produce the characteristics of the organism. The
nose-cell's genes have already been activated, the messanger and transfer
RNA and the amino acids with their peptide linkages have already been
created and processed.

The only viable cell to use for reproduction is an 'undifferentiated',
i.e. unprocessed and unexpressed cell. 

Kennita also says:
>BTW -- I talked to my gynecologist this week, and he said that part of
>the reason that eggs aren't frozen more often is that they would need
>to be ripened first, then collected one by one; it doesn't work well
>to take them directly out of the ovary while they're still "green",
>because we don't know what's involved in the maturing them inside the
>ovary.  

[BTW, Thanks, Kennita, for the info.]
The solution to this seems simple to me.  Freeze the egg(s) in their
'unripe' stage, and when the woman is ready to have them fertilized,
put them back in her ovary where she can 'ripen' them.  This means
that test-tube fertilization may not work for thawed (from frozen) eggs,
but we have still have that tried and true method, sex, to fertilize
them, right?

Amara Graps
