enhancement in many cases, although they will be heavily biased
towards bringing sub-normal performance into the normal range.

> Well-designed genes, available on the free market, would
> make transhuman children possible, with possibly devastating
> results for the State,

This would likely speed up technological progress, but I
don't see that it would necessarily have much impact on the
State.  Smart children can grow up to be better tax collectors
or better tax evaders, cops or victimless "criminals", socialists or 
libertarians, etc.  Now if for some reason libertarians
have many more and smarter children than socialists, and said
children remain libertarians, that might make a big difference
in the long run.  That's not the current demographic.

Reilly Jones wrote:
> >  The loving connection of two parents and child, supported
> >by pro-family societal values, does more to create healthy,
> >intelligent people than any genetic tinkering or screening
> >or adoption schemes ever will.  The bulk of the self is
> >epigenetically derived.

I think you meant "environmentally derived".  "Epigenetic" traits are 
those traits caused by the genes.  In reality, both play a strong 
role in development.  In an earlier excellent posted, Jay Freeman 
noted current scientific consensues that c. 60% of the variance in 
IQ is epigenetic, not environmental.

The environmental factor might rise quite a bit with good 
medical enhancement.  It may well be that smart 
drugs and adult genetic therapy can provide as much enhancement 
as germ-line genetic therapy.  The combination may be best of all.

Romana:
> How about the loving connection of *four* parents and child?
> or *three* or *one*? Is it just the _love connection_ that
> counts, or are you arguing that number and gender of parents
> counts too?

Fundamentally it's the love connection at counts.  (Pre-)historically,
across human cultures, genetic relatives (almost always on the mother's 
side) and couples (usually biased towards the mother) have dominated child 
rearing.  There are some basic selfish-gene, epigenetic factors at 
work here.  One example of many: fathers are often very demotivated to
parent when they suspect cuckoldry, and such suspicion can often lead to
child abuse.  This area is fascinatingly illuminated in
Donald Symon's _The Evolution of Human Sexuality_, which
treat sexual behaviors in detail, combining Dawkin's self-gene theory 
(aka sociobiology), detailed accounts from the anthropological
literature, and sampling of the Western humanities.  

There is so much detail involved in these relationships, for which
science has only skimmed the surface, that I myself am quite reluctant 
to be a guinea pig with such an important part of my life.  I prefer to
observe and what has succeeded for others, and the "logically deep"
solutions various cultures have worked out for these problems over
the millenia.  When the human epigenetic biases of myself and partners
are engineered away, at that point there will be need for massive
experimentation, accompanied by massive forethought about what
motivates Extropian behavior in a posthuman state where emotions
and instincts we took for granted are gone.

> ...present evidence...

In general, it might be quite valuable to dig into the 
social science literature.  For example, a consensus seems to
be forming in contemporary culture that one-parent families are
bad news for children.  What do the well-done studies on this
conclude?

> Extropians are generally suspicious of arguments
> about "societal values" because they are often invoked to
> justify the persecution of individuals, especially in cases
> of consensual crime.

This suspicion is quite well-founded.  It is, however, orthogonal
to whether in fact we want to consent.

> "Green wood can be bent; when it is dry, it is straightened
> only by fire." - Sufi proverb

Interesting parallels to the incredible learning power of
a young child (intentional?).  Some neurologists now theorize
that the brain of a young child (up to age 5?),
is undergoing a fundamentally different and much
more flexible form of learning via neural and synaptic growth
and death, than later learning which involves only realignment of
existing synapses.  If so, this makes young children the
most anti-entropic, most efficient self-transforming
processes on the planet, until the day comes we can mimic 
these early-learning capabilities in old brains via advanced
genetic therapy.

BTW, this is a great thread!  Much more relevant to our
lives as we live them today than the moral status of the poor, 
our wishes for radically changing the world's political
landscape, space colonies, meany vs. wimp wars, etc., in my 
humble opinion.

