Date:     Mon, 27 Dec 93 15:19:38 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Why have children?

My best guess about the effect of parents being able to select genetic 
traits for their children is that most parents in the US will 
choose health for their children, but they won't especially choose
intelligence, though they will select against retardation.

What I'm looking at is what sort of self-transformation people
seem to want for themselves, and what they support in terms of
schools. This suggests that what most people (in the US) want is 
"good" looks and atheletic ability. (Anyone have similar guesses
about what traits might be popular in other countries or cultures?)

I'm still in favor of access to genetic engineering--and look forward
to the day when people can choose for themselves (through tailored
viruses or somesuch)--both because it's the only way that *I* can
make personal use of g.e. and because the ethical problems of choosing 
for one's children would become much smaller. 

Part of this is the opportunities for interesting changes that I
see for myself, but a lot of it is a desire to see the intelligent
life on this planet (eventually, it won't be a single "human race"
in the sense that we use the word now) become much more wildly
interesting and various. I'm not expecting to see optimization
for intelligence or any other well-defined trait. 

Actually, if we're just talking about survival/ability to influence
the future, maybe we should be talking about designing children
to be good at influencing other people while being willing to
take some losses on verbal/math intelligence if necessary, especially
if there are still suckers out there who are willing to raise the
technicians. 

It's all still evolution in action--it's just that genetic and
memetic evolution become intertwined in interesting ways.

Nancy Lebovitz

